PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The dominant credible answer emerging from round 0 is (201,301), supported by multiple independent, detailed arguments (colleagues 1,6,8,15,19 and others) that correctly apply the ℓ-cohesive threshold |N*| ≥ ℓ·n/100 to the fixed group of the original six voters. Their intersection size 3 forces ℓ=3 (hence PJR violation) exactly when n≤200; at n=201 the max admissible ℓ drops to 2, which is satisfiable by {y,z}. EJR additionally requires some single voter inside that group to personally obtain ℓ representatives; because each of the six obtains at most one from {y,z}, the same group remains 2-cohesive up to n=300, so EJR first becomes feasible only at n=301.  

Counter-arguments for smaller values (101,151,200) or identical values (151,151; 501,501; 601,601) rest on incorrect cohesive-group calculations, on treating singletons or five-voter subsets as the binding constraint, or on mis-applying the union vs. individual-representation distinction between PJR and EJR. Larger values such as (301,601) over-estimate the required n by ignoring that the six-voter group’s entitlement already falls to ℓ=2 (PJR) or ℓ=1 (EJR) at the 201/301 thresholds.  

No unresolved technical questions remain; the reasoning is fully determined by the given six ballots plus the standard definitions. The pair is therefore (201,301).}

